// ═══ BASIC: auto-close on exit ═══
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("trades.csv"))) {
    String line = reader.readLine();
    // use reader...
}
// reader.close() called automatically HERE — even if exception occurred!
// No finally block needed. No null check needed.

// ═══ MULTIPLE resources: closed in REVERSE order ═══
try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
     PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM trades");
     ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery()) {
    while (rs.next()) {
        // process row
    }
}
// Closed in REVERSE: rs.close() → stmt.close() → conn.close()
// Reverse ensures: inner resources closed before outer (correct dependency order)

// ═══ CUSTOM AutoCloseable ═══
public class DistributedLock implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String lockKey;

    public DistributedLock(String lockKey) {
        this.lockKey = lockKey;
        acquireLock(lockKey);  // lock acquired on creation
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        releaseLock(lockKey);  // lock released automatically!
    }
}

try (DistributedLock lock = new DistributedLock("SETTLEMENT_LOCK")) {
    performSettlement();
}
// Lock ALWAYS released — even if settlement throws!

// ═══ SUPPRESSED exceptions ═══
try (AutoCloseable resource = () -> { throw new Exception("CLOSE_FAIL"); }) {
    throw new RuntimeException("MAIN_ERROR");
}
// Caller catches: RuntimeException("MAIN_ERROR")  ← PRIMARY
// e.getSuppressed()[0]: Exception("CLOSE_FAIL")    ← SUPPRESSED (not lost!)
// BOTH visible! Unlike finally-throw which SWALLOWS the real error.

// ═══ BEFORE (verbose finally pattern) ═══
Connection conn = null;
try {
    conn = dataSource.getConnection();
    // use connection
} catch (SQLException e) {
    log.error("Error", e);
} finally {
    if (conn != null) {
        try { conn.close(); }
        catch (SQLException e) { log.warn("Close failed", e); }
    }
}
// 12 lines of boilerplate!

// ═══ AFTER (try-with-resources) ═══
try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
    // use connection
} catch (SQLException e) {
    log.error("Error", e);
}
// 4 lines! Clean! No finally. No null check. Auto-close guaranteed.
```

Java 9 enhancement — effectively final variables:

```java
// Java 7-8: resource MUST be declared in try():
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"));
// try (reader) { }  → COMPILE ERROR in Java 7-8!

// Java 9+: effectively final variable can be used:
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"));
try (reader) {  // (Recommended) Java 9+: effectively final — allowed!
    String line = reader.readLine();
}
// reader.close() called automatically!
```

